Regarding @@ comment "@@ index passed in is not equal to the old array size", it's not clear what exactly this comment is referring to without further context from the method. The best analysis can take into consideration the `AssumePrecondition` which seems to be ensuring that index and array.Count are in the expected range. 

For debugging purposes, instead of auto-generating an assert statement, what you can do is insert an explicit checkpoint or `Debug.Assert` where you understand better the assumptions and conditions for your code, or you can simply use `Debug.Assert` as needed throughout your code or as a default when writing tests. This lets you verify correctness of the behavior of your code whenever assumptions are violated. 

However, note that using Debug.Assert is not always an ideal solution as it breaks your code when in Release mode due to the existence of the Debug.Assert. Use your debugging tools for debugging purposes. And remember, your code is for the user and automated checks are just for you to verify your assumptions before the user arrives.

As a result, in this case an assert statement like this: `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != index, "index has been passed equal to the old array size");` can be used, but that would be inaccurate without further context about what it means that Index is supposed to be equal to the old array size. 

Automated tooling and human understanding always come into the picture on what the comment wants the assert statement to do.--------------------